<h1>TLS 1.3 with SLH-DSA: An Engineer’s Walkthrough</h1>

# TLS 1.3 with SLH-DSA: An Engineer’s Walkthrough

<p class="meta">Crypto & Network Security Blog · Post‑Quantum TLS · Practical Engineering</p>

<p>
TLS 1.3 was designed with a very clean separation of concerns: key exchange,
authentication, and record protection are mostly independent. That design
choice turns out to be extremely useful when experimenting with new
cryptographic primitives.
</p>

<p>
RFC&nbsp;9909 builds on this flexibility and shows how
<strong>SLH‑DSA</strong>, a post‑quantum, hash‑based signature scheme, can be
used for authentication in TLS&nbsp;1.3. Importantly, this does <em>not</em>
change the structure of the handshake. From the protocol’s point of view,
SLH‑DSA is just another signature algorithm.
</p>

<h2>Where SLH‑DSA Fits in the TLS Handshake</h2>

<p>
TLS uses digital signatures in exactly two places during the handshake:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>To authenticate the server (and optionally the client)</li>
  <li>To bind the handshake transcript to the authenticated identity</li>
</ul>

<p>
SLH‑DSA replaces ECDSA or RSA in those steps. Everything else—key exchange,
HKDF, record encryption—stays unchanged.
</p>

<div class="figure">
<pre class="diagram">$1</pre>
<div class="figcaption">Figure 4 — TLS CertificateVerify with SLH-DSA</div>
</div>

<p>
The handshake messages are identical to a classical TLS 1.3 handshake. The
only difference is the algorithm used to generate and verify the signature in
<code>CertificateVerify</code>.
</p>

<h2>TLS Handshake State Machine (Authentication View)</h2>

<p>
From an implementation perspective, it helps to think in terms of states.
Below is the authentication‑relevant slice of the TLS 1.3 state machine.
Nothing here is specific to SLH‑DSA—the algorithm plugs into an existing
transition.
</p>

<pre class="diagram">
[Start]
   |
   v
[ClientHello Sent]
   |
   v
[ServerHello Recvd]
   |
   v
[Certificate Recvd]
   |
   v
[Verify Certificate Signature]
   |
   v
[Handshake Finished]
</pre>

<p>
The verification step simply switches from ECDSA/RSA logic to SLH‑DSA
verification. The surrounding protocol logic remains untouched.
</p>

<h2>Why SLH‑DSA?</h2>

<p>
SLH‑DSA is part of the NIST post‑quantum standardization effort. It is based
entirely on hash functions and avoids algebraic structures like lattices or
elliptic curves.
</p>

<p>
That design makes it conservative. If your threat model includes long‑term
harvest‑now‑decrypt‑later attacks, this conservatism can be attractive.
</p>

<h2>Engineering Tradeoffs: SLH‑DSA vs Dilithium</h2>

<p>
In practice, SLH‑DSA is rarely the first post‑quantum signature engineers reach
for. Dilithium is usually the default choice. The reason is simple: tradeoffs.
</p>

<pre>
SLH‑DSA
  • Hash‑based, minimal assumptions
  • Very strong long‑term security story
  • Large public keys and signatures
  • Slower signing and verification

Dilithium
  • Lattice‑based design
  • Compact signatures
  • Fast verification
  • Newer hardness assumptions
</pre>

<p>
For most web‑scale TLS deployments, Dilithium is easier to deploy without
impacting latency or handshake size. SLH‑DSA makes more sense in environments
where long‑term cryptographic conservatism outweighs performance concerns.
</p>

<h2>Certificates and ASN.1 (What You Actually Need to Care About)</h2>

<p>
TLS implementations typically fail not in the handshake logic, but in
certificate parsing. SLH‑DSA keys are carried in X.509 certificates using
standard ASN.1 structures.
</p>

<p>
At a high level, the public key appears in <code>SubjectPublicKeyInfo</code>.
There are no algorithm parameters; the raw public key bytes are carried
directly.
</p>

<pre>
SubjectPublicKeyInfo ::= SEQUENCE {
  algorithm         AlgorithmIdentifier,
  subjectPublicKey  BIT STRING
}

AlgorithmIdentifier ::= SEQUENCE {
  algorithm   id-slh-dsa-256s-shake,
  parameters  ABSENT
}
</pre>

<p>
If you are implementing support, pay close attention to length handling and
BIT STRING parsing. Most real‑world bugs show up here.
</p>

<h2>Operational Considerations</h2>

<p>
Deploying SLH‑DSA in TLS has immediate operational consequences:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Larger handshake messages</li>
  <li>Higher CPU cost during authentication</li>
  <li>No impact on symmetric crypto or record protection</li>
</ul>

<p>
Because TLS 1.3 authenticates early, the cost is paid once per connection. In
many deployments, that cost is acceptable if connections are long‑lived.
</p>

<h2>Takeaway</h2>
<p>
RFC&nbsp;9909 demonstrates that TLS&nbsp;1.3 possesses strong cryptographic
agility without sacrificing protocol stability. The integration of SLH-DSA
is confined to the authentication path and does not affect key establishment
or record-layer protection.
</p>

<p>
From an implementation perspective, supporting SLH-DSA primarily involves
algorithm negotiation, certificate parsing, and signature verification logic.
No modifications to the core TLS state machine are required.
</p>

<h2>Extended Analysis and Design Discussion</h2>
<p>
This section expands the discussion to cover protocol context, design
rationale, implementation considerations, and operational tradeoffs in
sufficient depth to support archival and instructional use. The presentation
follows conventions commonly found in IEEE and ACM conference papers.
</p>

<h2>Background: TLS 1.3 Design Goals</h2>
<p>
TLS 1.3 was standardized with explicit goals of reducing handshake latency,
removing legacy cryptographic constructions, and improving formal
verifiability. The protocol minimizes round trips, encrypts more handshake
messages, and enforces a clean separation between key establishment,
authentication, and record protection.
</p>

<div class="figure">
<pre class="diagram">
Client                                   Server
------                                   ------
ClientHello        -------------------->
                    ServerHello
                    EncryptedExtensions
                    Certificate
                    CertificateVerify
                    Finished       <--------------------
Finished             -------------------->
</pre>
<div class="figcaption">Figure 5 — Baseline TLS 1.3 Full Handshake</div>
</div>

<p>
This separation is central to algorithm agility. Signature schemes can be
replaced without modifying the key schedule or record-layer cryptography.
</p>

<h2>Post-Quantum Threat Model</h2>
<p>
The post-quantum threat model assumes an adversary capable of recording
encrypted traffic today and decrypting it in the future using a cryptanalytically
relevant quantum computer. While TLS 1.3 already mitigates many passive
attacks, classical public-key signatures remain vulnerable under this model.
</p>

<p>
Post-quantum authentication aims to preserve the long-term authenticity of
recorded sessions, even if private keys are compromised by future advances
in computation.
</p>

<h2>SLH-DSA Overview</h2>
<p>
SLH-DSA is a stateless hash-based digital signature algorithm standardized in
NIST FIPS 205. Its security relies solely on the properties of cryptographic
hash functions, avoiding algebraic assumptions used by lattice-based or
elliptic-curve schemes.
</p>

<div class="figure">
<pre class="diagram">
Private Key  -->  Hash-Based Tree Structure  -->  Signature
Public Key   -->  Root Hash
</pre>
<div class="figcaption">Figure 6 — High-Level Structure of SLH-DSA</div>
</div>

<h2>Integration of SLH-DSA into TLS 1.3</h2>
<p>
RFC 9909 specifies how SLH-DSA is identified and negotiated in TLS. The
integration is deliberately minimal, affecting only the signature algorithm
registry and certificate handling logic.
</p>

<div class="figure">
<pre class="diagram">
Handshake Transcript
        |
        v
  Hash(Transcript)
        |
        v
   SLH-DSA.Sign
        |
        v
 CertificateVerify
</pre>
<div class="figcaption">Figure 7 — CertificateVerify Signing Flow with SLH-DSA</div>
</div>

<h2>TLS State Machine with Post-Quantum Authentication</h2>
<p>
From an implementation perspective, TLS is best modeled as a state machine.
Post-quantum authentication introduces no new states; it only alters the
verification logic at an existing transition.
</p>

<div class="figure">
<pre class="diagram">
[START]
   |
   v
CLIENT_HELLO_SENT
   |
   v
SERVER_HELLO_RECEIVED
   |
   v
CERTIFICATE_RECEIVED
   |
   v
CERTIFICATE_VERIFY (SLH-DSA)
   |
   v
HANDSHAKE_COMPLETE
</pre>
<div class="figcaption">Figure 8 — TLS State Machine with SLH-DSA Authentication</div>
</div>

<h2>ASN.1 Encoding and Certificate Processing</h2>
<p>
SLH-DSA public keys are carried in X.509 certificates using the
<code>SubjectPublicKeyInfo</code> structure. No algorithm parameters are
permitted, simplifying parsing but increasing sensitivity to length and
encoding errors.
</p>

<div class="figure">
<pre class="diagram">
SubjectPublicKeyInfo ::= SEQUENCE {
  algorithm         AlgorithmIdentifier,
  subjectPublicKey  BIT STRING
}

AlgorithmIdentifier ::= SEQUENCE {
  algorithm   id-slh-dsa-256s-shake,
  parameters  ABSENT
}
</pre>
<div class="figcaption">Figure 9 — ASN.1 Encoding of SLH-DSA Public Keys</div>
</div>

<h2>Performance Considerations</h2>
<p>
The primary costs associated with SLH-DSA are signature size and verification
time. These costs directly impact handshake bandwidth and CPU utilization,
particularly for short-lived TLS connections.
</p>

<h2>Comparative Analysis: SLH-DSA and Dilithium</h2>
<p>
While both SLH-DSA and Dilithium are standardized post-quantum signature
schemes, they represent different points in the design space.
</p>

<div class="figure">
<pre class="diagram">
Security Assumptions
   |
   +--> Hash Functions (SLH-DSA)
   |
   +--> Lattice Problems (Dilithium)
</pre>
<div class="figcaption">Figure 10 — Security Assumption Comparison</div>
</div>

<p>
SLH-DSA prioritizes conservative assumptions and long-term confidence, while
Dilithium offers significantly better performance characteristics for
high-throughput environments.
</p>

<h2>Operational Deployment Models</h2>
<p>
Real-world deployments may adopt hybrid strategies, using post-quantum
signatures selectively or in combination with classical schemes during
transition periods.
</p>

<div class="figure">
<pre class="diagram">
Client Capability Set
   |
   +--> Classical Only
   +--> PQ Hybrid
   +--> PQ-Only
</pre>
<div class="figcaption">Figure 11 — Deployment Capability Profiles</div>
</div>

<h2>Security Analysis Summary</h2>
<p>
The integration defined by RFC 9909 preserves TLS 1.3 security invariants.
No additional attack surfaces are introduced beyond those inherent to the
chosen signature scheme.
</p>

<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>
This extended discussion demonstrates that TLS 1.3 provides a robust
foundation for post-quantum authentication. SLH-DSA can be integrated with
minimal protocol disruption, enabling incremental migration strategies that
align with operational realities.
</p>

<h2>References</h2>
<ol>
